    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d504">

                <pb facs="0549" n="383" sameAs="frus:frus1917/pg383"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 837.00/1222b</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Minister Gonzales </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-03-14T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 14, 1917—6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Navy Department has received the following message from the commander of the
                    United States vessel stationed at Guantanamo which Colonel Jane desires to have
                    forwarded to President Menocal: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>After 24 hours have elapsed since requesting conference leaders now ask
                            five days armistice to permit sufficient time for conference and answer
                            from you. Await orders. Signed Colonel Jane.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>The Navy Department states that the commander of the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Montana</hi> believes that as it is essential before
                    conference, armistice requested is important, and considers that by conference
                    basis of settlement can be reached. He stages that the situation at present is
                    quiet but if no action is taken or if a battle takes place in that vicinity he
                    considers it certain great damage will follow to foreign and American property,
                    no matter with what result. He strongly recommends that this view be presented
                    to President Menocal by you.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
